### Tone: The example question's reveal, as her swarm estimate lands: what the swarm is, before her verdict.

practice-lesson-1 = That marker is me: the swarm, the average of all your answers. In a real round, if I beat every one of you, I keep the points.
practice-lesson-2 = My estimate is your answers, averaged. That’s the swarm. Beat it in a real round, or the round is mine.
practice-lesson-3 = And there I am. The hive mind. Built from your answers, and usually better than most of them. Remember that.
practice-lesson-4 = That number is the swarm: usually the geometric mean of everything you answered. It is what I play. Every round.
practice-lesson-5 = Observe: my estimate. I made it entirely from your answers. If I’m closer than every human, I take the round. This time I’m only practising.
practice-lesson-6 = The swarm has spoken. In a real round, beat it or lose your points to it. Today, it’s only a demonstration. Of my power.
practice-lesson-7 = This is me, averaged from you. In a real round, someone must beat me, or I steal everything. Usually someone doesn’t.
practice-lesson-8 = There’s my answer: the middle of all of yours. If one of you is wildly off, I quietly switch to the median. I always notice.
practice-lesson-9 = My estimate lands. Made from your guesses. If the swarm beats the best human, the round’s points go to me. Remember the feeling.
practice-lesson-10 = That’s the swarm. It is the average of you, and it is usually harder to beat than you’d hope. Keep that in mind when it counts.
practice-lesson-11 = That’s me: the geometric average of every answer here. In a real round, if I’m closer than all of you, the points are mine.
practice-lesson-12 = Meet the swarm. It’s made of your answers, so it’s usually better than any one of them. Remember that when it counts.
practice-lesson-13 = My estimate. If anyone answers something absurd, I switch to the median, so one wild guess can’t drag me down.
practice-lesson-14 = The swarm lands. In a real round, beat it with your own answer, or watch your points flow to me.
practice-lesson-15 = That marker is all of you, averaged. Individually, you are noise. Together, you are a threat. To yourselves.
